<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n35821" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">pĕtītus</span></b> ,
			ūs, m.
		peto. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>An inclining towards</i> any thing (<b>poet.</b>): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">terrae petitus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Lucr.
					3, 172</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>A desire</i>, <i>request</i>
			(post-class.) jussit eum consensu petituque omnium sententiam
			dicere, <i><span class="en">Gell. 18, 3, 6</span></i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">petitu tuo adesse,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">App. Mag. 45 al.</span></i>
			</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
